WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.231
Hi, I'm Hassock!

00:00:02.231 --> 00:00:05.997
DeepSeek shipped a new Flash model five weeks after the last one.

00:00:05.997 --> 00:00:08.507
I opened both repos to see what changed.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.645
The config, the tokenizer, the code and every tensor shape are the same.

00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:16.329
Only the weights moved, and all of them moved.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.856
A prompt only runs through the first twenty layers, the encoder.

00:00:21.856 --> 00:00:25.580
That keeps prefill to about eight billion active parameters a token.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.457
Two giant n-gram tables feed the encoder.

00:00:28.457 --> 00:00:33.372
The model hashes the last few tokens and looks rows up instead of multiplying.

00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:37.756
The decoder's cache is projected from the encoder's output, just eight hundred

00:00:37.756 --> 00:00:39.407
and ninety bytes a token.

00:00:39.827 --> 00:00:43.528
The decoder reads that cache to write each new token, with six

00:00:43.528 --> 00:00:45.995
of three hundred and eighty-four experts per layer.

00:00:47.425 --> 00:00:49.565
Every row on the card went up.

00:00:49.565 --> 00:00:53.844
The biggest jump is Terminal-Bench, though the new run used a different agent scaffold.

00:00:55.733 --> 00:01:00.098
Against Opus five, the new model loses seven rows, including the hardest

00:01:00.098 --> 00:01:02.280
reasoning exam by a wide margin.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.449
It wins four, all agentic rows.

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:08.237
On par is fair for agent work, and a stretch for the rest.

00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:10.962
DeepSeek's own chart.

00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:14.943
At max effort the old model cost about forty-nine cents a task.

00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:17.928
The new one, about twenty cents, and scores higher.

00:01:19.258 --> 00:01:22.244
As shipped, the model is twice what two Sparks hold.

00:01:22.244 --> 00:01:25.528
Put the lookup tables on disk, squeeze the experts below three

00:01:25.528 --> 00:01:26.722
bits, and it fits.

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:29.579
And here is the surprise.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:33.530
Each token reads more dense weight than expert weight, so squeezing the

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.176
experts barely speeds up decoding.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.001
Same model, new weights.

00:01:38.001 --> 00:01:39.352
Strong on agent work.

00:01:39.352 --> 00:01:41.716
And two Sparks, only after a re-encode.

00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:44.184
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.184 --> 00:01:44.769
I'm Hassock.

00:01:44.769 --> 00:01:45.062
Bye!
